# Cluster certificate artifacts

Certificates for the parts of this environment that this repo provisions but
does not configure: the mirror registry, and the OpenShift API and ingress.
All are signed by the sandbox root CA (`../sandbox-ca.crt`), so a single
trust bundle covers every TLS endpoint the cluster will encounter.

Generated for **{{ cluster_domain }}**. Regenerated automatically if deleted;
a re-run leaves existing files alone.

| File | Used by |
|---|---|
| `registry.crt` / `registry.key` | mirror-registry `--sslCert` / `--sslKey` |
| `api.crt` / `api.key` | `APIServer` named certificate |
| `ingress.crt` / `ingress.key` | `IngressController` default certificate |
| `install-config-additionalTrustBundle.yaml` | install-config fragment |

Keys are `0600`. The whole `artifacts/` tree is gitignored.

## Mirror registry

```bash
./mirror-registry install \
  --quayHostname registry.{{ cluster_domain }} \
  --sslCert registry.crt \
  --sslKey registry.key
```

## install-config.yaml

Paste the contents of `install-config-additionalTrustBundle.yaml`. This is
what lets the installer and every node verify the mirror registry — without
it a disconnected install fails during bootstrap with image pull errors that
never mention certificates.

It belongs alongside `imageContentSources`, which this file does not cover
because the mirror set depends on what you actually mirrored.

## API server certificate

Applied after the cluster is up, not during install:

```bash
oc create secret tls api-cert \
  --cert=api.crt --key=api.key -n openshift-config

oc patch apiserver cluster --type=merge -p '{
  "spec": {"servingCerts": {"namedCertificates": [{
    "names": ["api.{{ cluster_domain }}"],
    "servingCertificate": {"name": "api-cert"}
  }]}}}'
```

Note the SAN list includes `api-int.{{ cluster_domain }}`, the in-cluster
name the control plane uses to reach itself. Replacing the API certificate
without it breaks internal clients.

## Ingress certificate

```bash
oc create secret tls ingress-cert \
  --cert=ingress.crt --key=ingress.key -n openshift-ingress

oc patch ingresscontroller.operator default -n openshift-ingress-operator \
  --type=merge -p '{"spec":{"defaultCertificate":{"name":"ingress-cert"}}}'
```

## Validity

{{ pki_cert_validity }} from issue. Apple rejects TLS server certificates
valid for more than 398 days, so these are deliberately under that — a
longer-lived certificate works from Linux and fails in Safari and Chrome.

To rotate, delete the `.crt` and re-run. Changing `pki_cert_validity` alone
does **not** reissue: `community.crypto` ignores timestamp drift by design,
or relative validity would churn on every run.
